  "hints": [
    "Start from the traffic: half of it is static. What absorbs static files before they reach your servers?",
    "Split the API: a Catalog & Orders service (cache in front of the database, a queue for order events) and a separate Search Service backed by a search engine, both behind the load balancer.",
    "Checkout races for the same stock: connect a Redis Lock (2 replicas) to both services. Two of everything stateful (LB, cache, DB, lock, search) removes single points of failure."
  ],
  "scoring": {
    "weights": {
      "performance": 0.35,
      "reliability": 0.3,
      "cost": 0.15,
      "architecture": 0.2
    },
    "latencyTargetMs": 50,
    "costBudget": 1800
  },
  "explanation": [
    "The biggest wins come from routing each kind of traffic to the cheapest thing that can serve it: CDN for static files, cache for hot product pages, search engine for keyword queries.",
    "Search is its own service because inverted indexes answer \"red running shoes\" in milliseconds, while LIKE '%red%' on SQL scans the whole table.",
    "Checkout is the one place correctness beats speed: lock the item (or use a conditional UPDATE \u2026 WHERE stock > 0) so the last unit sells exactly once.",
    "Order confirmation emails don't need to block checkout. Put an event on a queue and let workers send the email."
  ]
